# Citation metadata for The Intention Gap.
# Publishing name confirmed by the author. The MIT code license applies to software;
# original research documentation is separately CC BY 4.0 (LICENSE-docs.md).
# No release date, ORCID, affiliation, or DOI has been claimed.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this project, please cite it as below. It is an independent research project and has
  not been peer reviewed. Please also cite the original data sources listed under references.
type: software
title: "The Intention Gap: Will Generation Z have the families they say they want?"
version: "1.0.0-rc1"
repository-code: "https://github.com/seanthengineer/the-intention-gap"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  An independent research project combining four designs to examine U.S. fertility intentions and
  outcomes: stated birth expectations against observed births in the NLSY97 (women born 1980-84),
  wanting and intending children among adults aged about 18-27 in the 2022-2023 NSFG, rental cost
  burden and state birth rates (ACS and CDC WONDER, 2014-2024), and forecasts of the U.S. total
  fertility rate and Generation Z cohort fertility to 2040 from the Human Fertility Database. It
  concludes that whether Generation Z will achieve its desired family size cannot be established
  from existing data. Results are frozen by SHA-256 manifests and independently recomputed.
authors:
  - name: "Sean Nathaniel M."
keywords:
  - demography
  - fertility
  - fertility intentions
  - Generation Z
  - survey statistics
  - forecasting
  - reproducible research
